  m_Name: Schistosomiasis Japonica
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::MedicalCase
  caseImage1: {fileID: -5893205898659303077, guid: f8899c6d92d122b4987dd6f4ecaab017, type: 3}
  caseImage2: {fileID: -1644715245136685197, guid: 2094c60e3746b1346b6ca96c26ffeee9, type: 3}
  title: Schistosomiasis Japonica
  type: Uncommon
  category: Infectious - Parasitic
  description: A parasitic infection acquired when the skin comes into contact with
    freshwater containing infective parasites. It is an ongoing endemic public-health
    issue in selected Philippine communities and involves both human and animal reservoirs.
  symptoms: 'Abdominal discomfort and pain that comes and goes for months.   Bowel
    changes including loose stools and blood in the stool.   Fatigue, weakness, loss
    of appetite, and weight loss. '
  pathology: ransmission is associated with repeated environmental exposure to contaminated
    freshwater, such as wading barefoot, bathing, or crossing rivers to reach farms.
  treatment: 'Proper clinical evaluation including appropriate stool tests and liver
    or blood-related assessments if indicated.   Connection with local health teams
    for guidance, treatment, and community-based screening.   Prevention involves
    using protective footwear or clothing for necessary work and avoiding unnecessary
    contact with unsafe freshwater. '
  keyFeatures: Recognized by connecting persistent gastrointestinal symptoms and
    bleeding with a relevant environmental history of freshwater exposure. It is
    critical to screen for danger signs like heavy or black bloody stool, fainting,
    confusion, yellowing of the eyes (jaundice), breathing trouble, and marked abdominal
    swelling.
  requiredStageLevel: 3
